The sorted array can also be searched by prefixes. For each bit, the current valid range is split into numbers with a `0` and numbers with a `1` at that bit. The side that gives a different bit from `x` is kept when it has at least one value.

The limit is handled first with `upper_bound`, so only values no greater than `m` are considered. After every bit, `candidate` stores the prefix of the number selected so far.

For example, if the current bit of `x` is `0`, numbers with a `1` are preferred. If that part is empty, the search stays in the `0` part.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> maximizeXor(vector<int>& nums, vector<vector<int> >& queries) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(queries.size());

        for (const vector<int>& query : queries) {
            int x = query[0];
            int limit = query[1];
            int end = upper_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), limit) - nums.begin();

            if (end == 0) {
                answer.push_back(-1);
                continue;
            }

            int left = 0;
            int right = end;
            int candidate = 0;

            for (int bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {
                int step = 1 << bit;
                int split = lower_bound(
                    nums.begin() + left,
                    nums.begin() + right,
                    candidate + step
                ) - nums.begin();

                int xBit = (x >> bit) & 1;

                if (xBit == 0) {
                    if (split < right) {
                        candidate += step;
                        left = split;
                    } else {
                        right = split;
                    }
                } else {
                    if (left < split) {
                        right = split;
                    } else {
                        candidate += step;
                        left = split;
                    }
                }
            }

            answer.push_back(candidate ^ x);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: sorting takes `O(n log n)`. Each query performs 31 binary searches, so the total is `O(n log n + 31q log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(q)` for the returned answers. This code sorts and changes `nums`, but does not change `queries`.

Sorting the numbers and the queries by their limit lets the trie contain exactly the values that are valid for the current query. A pointer adds each number only once.

During a trie search, the opposite bit is preferred because it gives a `1` in the XOR result. The original query index is saved so sorting does not change the output order.

Small dry run: with `nums = [0, 1, 2]` and query `[3, 1]`, only `0` and `1` enter the trie. The best result is `3 ^ 0 = 3`.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private static class TrieNode {
        TrieNode[] child = new TrieNode[2];
    }

    private void insert(TrieNode root, int value) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (int bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {
            int current = (value >> bit) & 1;
            if (node.child[current] == null) {
                node.child[current] = new TrieNode();
            }
            node = node.child[current];
        }
    }

    private int findMaximum(TrieNode root, int value) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        int result = 0;

        for (int bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {
            int current = (value >> bit) & 1;
            int wanted = current ^ 1;

            if (node.child[wanted] != null) {
                result |= (1 << bit);
                node = node.child[wanted];
            } else {
                node = node.child[current];
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    public List<Integer> maximizeXor(int[] nums, int[][] queries) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        int queryCount = queries.length;

        int[][] orderedQueries = new int[queryCount][3];
        for (int i = 0; i < queryCount; i++) {
            orderedQueries[i][0] = queries[i][0];
            orderedQueries[i][1] = queries[i][1];
            orderedQueries[i][2] = i;
        }

        Arrays.sort(orderedQueries, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>(Collections.nCopies(queryCount, -1));
        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
        int numberIndex = 0;

        for (int[] query : orderedQueries) {
            int x = query[0];
            int limit = query[1];

            while (numberIndex < nums.length && nums[numberIndex] <= limit) {
                insert(root, nums[numberIndex]);
                numberIndex++;
            }

            if (numberIndex > 0) {
                answer.set(query[2], findMaximum(root, x));
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n + q log q + 31(n + q))`.

Space complexity: `O(31n + q)`. Sorting changes the `nums` array, while the original query order is restored in the result.

This version builds a temporary trie only from values allowed by one query. It is not the fastest choice for large limits, but the condition is very clear: values larger than `m` are never inserted.

The trie search greedily tries the opposite bit from `x`. If the trie stays empty, the query gets `-1`.

Dry run: for `x = 5` and allowed values `[1, 2]`, the trie compares both bit paths and returns the larger of `5 ^ 1` and `5 ^ 2`.

