Time complexity: `O(q * (n + 31))`, which is `O(nq)` for large inputs.

Space complexity: `O(31n)` for the temporary trie. The input lists are not modified. This is best kept for small test cases or as a simple reference implementation.

For each query, I scan all values in `nums`. A value is allowed only when it is no greater than the query limit. Among the allowed values, the largest XOR is kept.

This is easy to follow and handles the case where no number matches by leaving the answer as `-1`. It is mainly useful for small inputs because it checks every array value for every query.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> maximizeXor(vector<int>& nums, vector<vector<int> >& queries) {
        vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(queries.size());

        for (const vector<int>& query : queries) {
            int x = query[0];
            int limit = query[1];
            int best = -1;

            for (int value : nums) {
                if (value <= limit) {
                    best = max(best, x ^ value);
                }
            }

            answer.push_back(best);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * q)`, where `n` is the size of `nums` and `q` is the number of queries.

Space complexity: `O(q)` for the answer list. The input array is not changed.

